startup-title = RustTerm could not start
startup-version = Version: { $version } ({ $build })
startup-build-development = development build
startup-build-release = release build
startup-config-directory = Configuration directory: { $path }
startup-details = Details: { $error }
startup-log = Diagnostic log: { $path }
startup-incompatible = This version cannot read this configuration. It may have been saved by a newer release or a development build.
startup-incompatible-help = Use the version that saved the configuration, or upgrade RustTerm. Back up your data before switching versions; do not delete the configuration to fix this error.
startup-config-invalid = The configuration or saved workspace could not be loaded. The file may be damaged or incompatible with this version.
startup-config-help = Check the file identified below or restore a known-good backup. RustTerm has not replaced the unreadable file with defaults.
startup-already-running = Another RustTerm instance is using this configuration directory. Development and release builds use the same directory by default.
startup-already-running-help = Return to the running instance or close it before trying again. Use --config-dir with a different directory for an independent instance.
startup-storage = RustTerm could not access its data directory.
startup-storage-help = Check that the directory is accessible and owned by your account. The details below identify the affected path.
startup-other = RustTerm stopped before its main window could open.
startup-other-help = Check the error details below. When starting from a desktop shortcut, you can also run RustTerm in a terminal to see its diagnostic output.
connections-unrecognized-configuration-data = Configuration contains an unrecognized setting or type at byte offset { $offset }; it may require a newer RustTerm version.
